🧠 SLAB_UAF_DETECTOR.C — SIMPLE EXPLANATION (LAYMAN FRIENDLY)

Think of this program like a "security guard system inside a warehouse (RAM)"
that stores and tracks boxes (memory objects).

Each box is carefully monitored so that:
- nobody uses it after throwing it away
- nobody throws it away twice
- nobody secretly corrupts it


===========================================================
🏭 REAL WORLD ANALOGY
===========================================================

RAM (Memory)        = Warehouse
struct uaf_object   = Box / Package
SLAB cache          = Shelf system (organized storage)
RCU                 = Delayed cleanup truck
procfs              = Control dashboard (like CCTV screen)
trace_printk        = Security camera logs


===========================================================
📦 WHAT EACH BOX CONTAINS
===========================================================

Each object (box) has:

┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ canary_start  (security tag) │
│ id            (box number)    │
│ name          (label)        │
│ freed         (status flag)  │
│ rcu           (delayed free) │
│ list          (inventory)    │
│ canary_end    (security tag) │
└──────────────────────────────┘


👉 "Canary" = safety seal
If broken → memory corruption detected


===========================================================
🔁 FULL LIFE CYCLE OF AN OBJECT
===========================================================

STEP 1: ALLOCATION (CREATE BOX)

   System:
   "Give me a new box"

   Action:
   - SLAB allocates memory
   - object is initialized
   - added to tracking list

   RESULT:

   [ NEW OBJECT CREATED ]


STEP 2: USE OBJECT

   access_object()

   System checks:
   - Is it valid?
   - Is it corrupted?

   If OK:
   → allow access


STEP 3: FREE OBJECT (REMOVE BOX)

   free_object()

   System:
   - marks object as "freed"
   - removes from list
   - DOES NOT delete immediately

   Instead:
   → waits for safe time (RCU)


STEP 4: RCU CLEANUP (DELAYED DESTRUCTION)

   After safety delay:

   - memory is overwritten (0xA5 pattern)
   - memory is returned to SLAB

   This prevents crashes in other CPUs still using it


===========================================================
💥 USE AFTER FREE (CORE IDEA)

This line in your code:

    access_object(obj);   // AFTER free

Means:

👉 "Someone is trying to use a box that is already thrown away"


System detects:

🚨 USE-AFTER-FREE DETECTED
→ logs error
→ dumps stack trace


===========================================================
🧠 MEMORY VISUALIZATION (VERY IMPORTANT)

===========================================================

🟢 BEFORE ANYTHING:

   RAM (SLAB MEMORY POOL)
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │  Free space                  │
   │  Free space                  │
   │  Free space                  │
   └──────────────────────────────┘


===========================================================

🟡 AFTER ALLOCATION:

   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ [OBJ 1] ACTIVE               │
   │ [OBJ 2] ACTIVE               │
   │ free space                   │
   └──────────────────────────────┘

   Tracking systems:

   LIST:
   OBJ1 → OBJ2

   HASH (fast lookup):
   id=1 → OBJ1
   id=2 → OBJ2


===========================================================

🔴 AFTER FREE:

   You free OBJ1

   BUT memory is NOT immediately gone

   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ [OBJ 1] FREED (danger zone)  │  ⚠ still in memory
   │ [OBJ 2] ACTIVE               │
   │ free space                   │
   └──────────────────────────────┘


   LIST:
   OBJ2 only

   HASH:
   OBJ1 removed


BUT POINTER STILL EXISTS IN CODE
👉 THIS IS THE BUG ZONE


===========================================================

☠ USE AFTER FREE SCENARIO

   You do:

   access_object(obj1)


   But memory state is:

   - removed from system
   - still physically in RAM
   - may be overwritten later

   RESULT:

   🚨 kernel detects invalid use


===========================================================

🧪 CANARY PROTECTION (ANTI-CORRUPTION)

At edges of memory:

   [ CANARY ][ OBJECT DATA ][ CANARY ]

If attacker or bug overwrites memory:

   ❌ canary changes
   → "CORRUPTION DETECTED"


